 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <p>One or more questions for each outcome; the quiz in <a href="#balance">Weight and balance</a> and Figures 1 to 4 cover more.</p>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> Your departure is 8 hours away. Which briefing, and what then?</summary><p>An outlook briefing for trends now, then a standard briefing close to departure.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> Decode VRB04KT 1 1/2SM BR SCT005 OVC010 12/11 A3001 down to the ceiling.</summary><p>Wind variable at 4 kt, visibility 1½ miles in mist, scattered at 500 ft, overcast at 1,000 ft: the ceiling is 1,000 ft, because a scattered layer is not a ceiling.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> In Figure 2's TAF, what could you meet arriving at 0200Z on the 12th?</summary><p>Prevailing: broken at 8,000 ft and over 6 miles; but there is a 30% chance of 3 miles in thunderstorms and rain with cumulonimbus at 3,000 ft until 0400Z.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> Same field, same altimeter setting, but 20 °C warmer. What happens to pressure altitude and density altitude?</summary><p>Pressure altitude is unchanged; density altitude rises, so takeoff and landing distances grow and climb rate falls.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> Why might a downsloping runway be good for takeoff but bad for landing?</summary><p>Downslope helps acceleration on takeoff but lengthens the landing roll.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 5</span> In Figure 4, why does the CG move aft when the rear seat is empty?</summary><p>The rear-seat load sits forward of the CG, at {LOADING.items[1].arm} in; removing it takes away forward moment, so the balance point moves aft.</p></details>

 <h2 id="sources">Sources and applicability</h2>
 <h3>Sources (US civil, shared by US military pilots)</h3>
 <p id="source-phak"><strong>[1] Federal Aviation Administration, FAA-H-8083-25B, Pilot's Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge.</strong> <a href="https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/2022-03/pilot_handbook.pdf">PDF</a>, read 3 November 2026, by printed page: 8-7, 10-4 to 10-6, 11-3 to 11-4, 11-13 to 11-14, 13-5 to 13-9 and 16-17 to 16-18.</p>
 <p id="source-cfr"><strong>[2] 14 CFR part 91.</strong> The US civil fuel and alternate rules are summarised, with their source, in <a href={`${sortie}#reserves`}>Planning a Sortie</a>.</p>
 <h3>Military sources</h3>
 <p id="source-afh"><strong>[5] US Air Force and US Army, AFH 11-203 Volume 2 / TC 3-04.14-2, Weather for Aircrews — Products and Services, 13 August 2015, incorporating Change 5 of 13 December 2022.</strong> <a href="https://static.e-publishing.af.mil/production/1/af_a3/publication/afh11-203v2/afh11-203v2.pdf">Published on the Air Force e-Publishing site</a>, which states no releasability restrictions; read 3 November 2026 from a copy supplied for this revision, paragraphs 1.6.1 to 1.6.1.3 and Tables 1.1 and 1.2.</p>
 <p id="source-cnaf"><strong>[6] US Navy, CNAF M-3710.7, NATOPS General Flight and Operating Instructions Manual, 5 May 2016.</strong> Read 3 November 2026 from a copy supplied for this revision, paragraph 4.8.3, flight route weather briefing; summarised in this site's own words. Later editions may exist.</p>
 <h3>Sim sources</h3>
 <p id="source-bms"><strong>[3] Benchmark Sims, Falcon BMS 4.38 User Manual.</strong> <a href="https://cdn.falcon-bms.com/docs/4.38/BMS-User-Manual.pdf">PDF</a>, read 3 November 2026, pp. 48 and 55. A sim manual, cited only for the In the sim panel.</p>
 <p id="source-dcs"><strong>[4] Eagle Dynamics, DCS User Manual (English, 2020).</strong> <a href="https://www.digitalcombatsimulator.com/upload/iblock/ed6/87v22jwd1xh51i3rgki944xsf503istq/DCS_User_Manual_EN_2020.pdf">DCS User Manual (PDF)</a>, read 3 November 2026, pp. 112–113. A sim manual, cited only for the In the sim panel; later DCS versions changed the weather editor.</p>
 <p>Page-specific excerpts are recorded in the repository review evidence: <a href="https://github.com/burnzoire/oversweep/blob/main/docs/reviews/flight-planning-evidence.md">Flight Planning evidence</a>.</p>
 <p>Figures 1 to 4 use the FAA handbook's examples where stated; the other reports, the airfields, the trainer and its loads are invented.</p>
 <p class="note">Applicability: written for pilots of every service. The METAR and TAF codes and the performance methods are those of the FAA handbook, used by US military and civil pilots alike; other countries' codes differ slightly. Military weather briefs are from the Air Force and Army weather handbook and the Navy's general flight rules. Your service's weather rules, its weather unit's products and the aircraft's flight manual govern. This revision is Drafted pending independent source-backed review.</p>
